s are a bit different in  structure so at the beginning they have  a cell body rather than having receptors  for example this could be within the  central nervous system within the brain  let's say what it then has is this long  part here just like within the sensor in  your run that long part is called an  axon that are labeled in just a second  ok so just finish drawing this so this  long protrusion is called an axon so  let's say I want to move my finger these  dendrites first of all they carry  electrical impulses towards the cell  body so this is me wanting to move my  finger the electrical impulse this this  cell will be within the brain this cell  body the brain then the electrical  impulse will travel down the axon so  this thing here the muscle in my finger  and in this particular scenario the  muscle is referred to as an effector  this is just where the effect happens  the effect is to contract my muscle to  move it now  these those things there are called  dendrites remember dendrites take  electrical impulses towards the cell  body axon a is for axon a is for  electrical impulses away from the cell  body so the impulse is traveling down  that way  now the final thing we need to know  about motor neurons is they have these  things called a myelin sheath nice way  of remembering it it's Emmis for motor  Ms for myelin M is usually to do with  movement and is usually to do with  muscle so the myelin sheath insulates  the axon so we get nice fast  transmission for example Stephen Hawking  he had a disease called motor neuron  disease where the myelin sheath broke  down which means he imposes could not  travel down to his muscles meaning he  couldn't move however his sensory  neurons were fine so he could still see  and he could still touch and you can  still hear because these types of  neurons were fine they don't have my  think motor Milan the third type of  neuron and final type of neuron we need  to know about is a relay neuron  now the way I always remember the job of  a relay neuron is I think about a relay  race in a relay race a baton is passed  from one runner to the next if relay  neurone takes information from one  neuron to the next so what we have again  is we have these protrusions coming out  called dendrites now these dendrites  would be attached to sensory neurons  what we then have again that long  protrusion hopefully by now we know that  that's called an axon so this is where  the electrical signal passes down we  then have these connections here which  can then pass the signal on just like in  a relay race it can then pass that  signal on to a motor neuron so just to  put that into context let's say in a  reflex arc I put my hand on something  apart that would be detected by these  receptors in a sensory neuron and the  information would travel down here to  sew down this down draw down the axon to  a relay niall run one of these that  information would then go from the  dendrite towards the cell body down the  axon and then it would connect to a mote  in your run the information would travel  down the dendrite towards the cell body  then down this axon to the muscle in  order to move my finger away from this  stimulus that was causing it harm so  those are the three types of neuron  that's how they all look different from  each other that's their different jobs  and functions and that is all I'm going  to say on it thank you
 

